    "innovation": "This paper synthesizes recent observations, palaeoclimate records, and model simulations to argue that most observed high-latitude Southern Hemisphere climate trends are not unusual compared to past natural variability and are inconsistent with current climate models, which may underestimate natural variability or overestimate the forced response.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Southern Annular Mode",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Antarctica",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "The leading mode of atmospheric circulation variability in the SH high latitudes is the Southern Annular Mode (SAM). It is a measure of the mid-to-high-latitude atmospheric pressure gradi- ent and reflects the strength and position of the westerly winds that circle Antarctica. This in turn affects various aspects of Antarctic climate and controls the timing and distribution of rainfall received by the mid-latitude SH continents\u00b9\u2070.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "mid- to high-latitude SH",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "An almost equally important aspect of large-scale circulation variability in this region is the mid- to high-latitude response to tropical variability, particularly the El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO)\u00b9\u00b9.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "conductivity-temperature-depth tags",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MOUNTED_ON",
            "target_entity_instance": "elephant seals",
            "target_entity_type": "Platform",
            "paragraph": "The Argo profiling float pro- gramme and conductivity-temperature-depth tags mounted on elephant seals have provided substantial numbers of subsurface ocean profiles only since 2004 (ref. 7), and even now, few ocean profiles are obtained within the sea-ice zone.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Southern Annular Mode",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Antarctica",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Strengthening of the westerly winds associated with the positive SAM trend causes spatially coherent changes in SAT over Antarctica\u00b9\u2078, and in particular can account for the summer warming over the eastern Antarctic Peninsula\u00b9\u2079,\u00b2\u2070.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Southern Annular Mode",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Southern Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Cooling of the surface ocean and warming of the subsurface ocean\u00b2\u00b9\u207b\u00b2\u2075 throughout the Southern Ocean can also be partly attributed to a westerly wind-forced increase in northward Ekman transport of cold sub-Ant-arctic surface waters. Summer trends in the SAM are distinct from natural variations\u00b2\u2076, and are attributed to stratospheric ozone depletion, and the associated stratospheric cooling over Antarctica\u00b9\u2070,\u00b2\u2077.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Southern Annular Mode",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "West Antarctic Ice Sheet",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In addition, regional atmospheric circulation changes led to warming trends in winter and spring, distinct from the summertime warm- ing associated with the SAM, particularly over the WAIS and the western Antarctic Peninsula during the second half of the twentieth century \u00b9\u00b9,\u00b2\u2078\u207b\u00b3\u00b2.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Southern Annular Mode",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "western Antarctic Peninsula",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
